; ======================================================================
;  THERMAL.MOD - THE BCM2711's OWN TEMPERATURE MONITOR, AS A DRIVER
;  MODULE. THE FIRST REAL DRIVER TO LEAVE THE CORE.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  BUILD:
;      pmfc RaspberryPi4/Modules/thermal_avs.pi4 -t pi4 --module \
;           -o THERMAL.MOD
;
;  and copy THERMAL.MOD to the root of the boot medium beside
;  SETTINGS.TXT, with a line in MODULES.TXT naming it.
;
;  IT INCLUDES Anvil/Hal/seams.pbi AND NOTHING ELSE OF ANVIL'S. That is
;  the contract working rather than a coincidence: a module is separately
;  compiled, never sees the monitor source, and reaches the monitor only
;  through the service-table pointer it is handed. If this file ever
;  needs a second Anvil include, the contract has leaked and the leak is
;  the defect, not the include.
;
;  A TRAP WORTH AN OPERATOR'S AFTERNOON, MET WHILE WRITING THIS FILE. A
;  module build refused here with
;
;      COMPILER ERROR: Unexpected token 'pi4.thermal.avs'
;
;  pointing at the `Module` line, while the identical source built
;  perfectly from another directory. The cause was not this file and not
;  the compiler: pmfc reads `keywords.def` FROM THE SOURCE TREE in
;  preference to its own, and this repository's copy was three days stale
;  and predated the Module/ModuleABI/ModuleMatch/Seam keywords entirely.
;  A keyword the table does not carry is not a keyword, so the
;  declaration parsed as a bare identifier. The repository's copy is now
;  the compiler's; if this error ever returns, compare the two files
;  before reading another line of this one.
;
;  THIS FILE HOLDS NO ADDRESS OF ITS OWN. The board declares the device
;  and hands the driver its base as the second argument to probe and
;  init. That is what makes the claim "portable driver" checkable instead
;  of decorative: a module that kept a constant here would work perfectly
;  on this board and fail the first time the block moved, and nothing
;  would notice until it did.
;
;  WHAT IT IS FOR. It matches `brcm,bcm2711-thermal`, which is the
;  compatible string the vendor's own kernel driver binds to, and fills
;  the thermal seam's read function. With it loaded, Anvil's temperature
;  comes straight off the part; without it, Anvil falls back to the
;  firmware property interface exactly as it always did.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE REGISTER, AND WHERE ITS NUMBERS COME FROM
;
;  These facts moved here whole from RaspberryPi4/Lib/thermal.pi4 on
;  2026-09-10. They are the reason to trust the arithmetic below and they
;  travel with the code that uses them.
;
;  THE BLOCK. Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:68-77
;
;      avs_monitor: avs-monitor@7d5d2000 {
;          compatible = "brcm,bcm2711-avs-monitor", "syscon", "simple-mfd";
;          reg = <0x7d5d2000 0xf00>;
;          thermal: thermal { compatible = "brcm,bcm2711-thermal"; ... };
;      };
;
;  THE ADDRESS TRANSLATION IS THE DEVICE TREE'S OWN, and it is not the
;  familiar one. bcm2711.dtsi:40-42 gives the soc node three ranges:
;
;      <0x7e000000  0x0 0xfe000000  0x01800000>   the common peripherals
;      <0x7c000000  0x0 0xfc000000  0x02000000>   the BCM2711-only ones
;      <0x40000000  0x0 0xff800000  0x00800000>   the ARM-local block
;
;  0x7d5d2000 falls in the SECOND range, not the first, so it is
;  $FD5D2000 from the processor and not $FE5D2000. THAT TRANSLATION NOW
;  LIVES IN THE BOARD, in RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_mod.pi4's device table,
;  and arrives here as the `device` argument - which is the right place
;  for it, because it is a fact about how this SoC is wired rather than
;  about how the sensor works. The validity bits below are what would
;  catch a board that got it wrong.
;
;  THE REGISTER, ITS VALIDITY BITS AND ITS FIELD.
;  Datasheets/pi4/pwm/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711_thermal.c:24-26
;
;      #define AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS            0x200
;      #define AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS_VALID_MSK  (BIT(16) | BIT(10))
;      #define AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS_DATA_MSK   GENMASK(9, 0)
;
;  and its read (same file, lines 42-51) is exactly: read, refuse with
;  -EIO if EITHER validity bit is clear, mask to ten bits, convert.
;
;  THE CONVERSION IS A STRAIGHT LINE and its two coefficients are in the
;  device tree, not the driver. bcm2711.dtsi:626-629
;
;      &cpu_thermal { coefficients = <(-487) 410040>; ... };
;
;  which the driver reads as slope and offset and applies as
;
;      *temp = slope * val + offset;             (bcm2711_thermal.c:51)
;
;  in MILLIDEGREES - the comment one line above says so: "Convert a HW
;  code to a temperature reading (millidegree celsius)".
;
;  THE SLOPE IS NEGATIVE, which is the one thing about this sensor that
;  will trip a reader: a BIGGER code is a COLDER part. Code 0 would be
;  410 C and code 1023 would be -88 C, so the whole useful band sits
;  near the top of the range - about 739 at 50 C and 698 at 70 C. A
;  conversion written with the sign the other way round produces
;  plausible-looking numbers that move the wrong way, which is why the
;  gate checks the DIRECTION as well as the values.
;
;  HARDWARE-VERIFIED 2026-09-06 on a Pi 4: `fan` printed 39 C (39920
;  millidegrees) from this register with both validity bits set,
;  alongside the firmware's 85 C throttle point. The address, the two
;  validity bits and the negative slope are measured and not only cited.
;
;  READING THIS ADDRESS CANNOT HANG THIS BOARD. That is the Pi 4's
;  measured property, written down in RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_addr.pi4: no
;  address on this part is known to stall the interconnect, and an
;  address with nothing behind it returns a value rather than wedging -
;  unlike the Arduino Q's QCM2290, where exactly this kind of speculative
;  read cost a power cycle. So the register may be read before anything
;  has proved the AVS block is clocked, and the validity bits are what
;  turn a garbage answer into a refusal instead of into a temperature.
;  THAT IS ALSO WHY THE PROBE IS ALLOWED TO READ IT: a probe must be
;  side-effect-free, and on this part this read is.
; ======================================================================

Module "pi4.thermal.avs"
ModuleABI 1, 1
ModuleMatch "brcm,bcm2711-thermal"

; The published contract, and the only Anvil file a module may include.
XIncludeFile "Anvil/Hal/seams.pbi"

Seam #SVCCAP_THERMAL

#AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS   = $200
#AVS_TEMP_VALID_MASK  = $10400        ; BIT(16) | BIT(10)
#AVS_TEMP_DATA_MASK   = $3FF          ; bits 9:0

; The two coefficients, named separately so a reader can check each one
; against the device tree line rather than against a folded constant.
#AVS_TEMP_SLOPE  = -487
#AVS_TEMP_OFFSET = 410040

; The block base the board handed us at init. Zero until then, and a read
; before init answers the sentinel rather than dereferencing zero.
;
; GLOBALS IN A MODULE ARE INITIALISED ONCE, BEFORE THE FIRST LIFECYCLE
; ENTRY, by the compiler's own one-time initialiser - the module has no
; _start and no BSS-zeroing loop of its own, because it is entered on its
; caller's stack rather than on a machine coming out of reset.
Global gAvsBase.i = 0

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  AvsStatus(base) - the status word, unmasked, as an unsigned 32-bit
;  value. The barriers are the same pair the core file used: this is a
;  device register and the compiler is entitled to move an ordinary load.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i AvsStatus(base.i)
  Define v.i
  a64_barrier()
  v = PeekN(base + #AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS) & $FFFFFFFF
  a64_barrier()
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; 1 when BOTH validity bits are set. The vendor driver tests the pair
; together and so does this: bit 16 alone, or bit 10 alone, is not a
; reading its own code would use.
Procedure.i AvsValid(raw.i)
  If (raw & #AVS_TEMP_VALID_MASK) = #AVS_TEMP_VALID_MASK
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; The line, and nothing else. Separated from the read so a gate can drive
; it over the whole 0..1023 domain without a register model in the way.
Procedure.i AvsCodeToMilliC(code.i)
  ProcedureReturn #AVS_TEMP_SLOPE * (code & #AVS_TEMP_DATA_MASK) + #AVS_TEMP_OFFSET
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE SERVICE. This is the procedure whose address is published into the
;  thermal seam, and it is what Anvil calls on every temperature read.
;
;  IT TAKES NO ARGUMENT. The driver was handed its device once, at init,
;  and is not asked again - a seam function that had to be told its own
;  hardware on every call would push the driver's state back into the
;  monitor, which is the thing a module exists to stop.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalAvsRead()
  Define raw.i
  If gAvsBase = 0
    ProcedureReturn #MOD_TEMP_NONE
  EndIf
  raw = AvsStatus(gAvsBase)
  If AvsValid(raw) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #MOD_TEMP_NONE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn AvsCodeToMilliC(raw)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ModuleProbe(services, device) - is this device really here?
;
;  SIDE-EFFECT-FREE, AND ON THIS PART THAT IS PROVABLE RATHER THAN
;  PROMISED: the whole of it is one load from a status register that this
;  board has recorded as safe to read at any time. It writes nothing, it
;  stores nothing in its own globals, and it does not publish a service -
;  the monitor refuses a publish from outside init, so it could not even
;  if it tried.
;
;  A POSITIVE ANSWER CLAIMS THE DEVICE. Zero declines, and declining is a
;  normal outcome: the id matched but the block is not answering with a
;  valid reading, which is exactly what a probe is for. The loader then
;  leaves the seam empty and the monitor keeps using the firmware.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ModuleProbe(services.i, device.i)
  If device = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If AvsValid(AvsStatus(device)) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ModuleInit(services, device) - take the device and publish the service.
;
;  THE ONLY THING IT DOES BESIDES REMEMBERING THE BASE IS ONE CALL
;  THROUGH THE TABLE. The service-table pointer arrives in `services`;
;  slot #SVC_SEAM_SLOT_FILL of the core group holds SvcSeamFill, and the
;  address of it is read out and called. That is the whole of a module's
;  connection to the monitor.
;
;  IT RETURNS WHAT THE FILL RETURNED. A module that ignored a refused
;  fill and answered 0 would be reported ACTIVE while its seam stayed
;  empty; returning the code makes the loader unwind and say so.
;
;  THE BASE IS STORED ONLY AFTER THE FILL SUCCEEDS. If the publish is
;  refused the driver leaves itself exactly as it was found, so the
;  unwound record holds a module that has taken nothing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ModuleInit(services.i, device.i)
  Define *fill
  Define rc.i
  If services = 0 Or device = 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  ; A POINTER VARIABLE AND NOT A PLAIN .i, and the star is load-bearing.
  ; The compiler refuses `x(...)` on an ordinary integer that was never
  ; assigned @Procedure - "nothing has ever stored an address in it, so
  ; that branch goes wherever its current value happens to point" - which
  ; is exactly the defect that once sent a board to BOOTSEL with nothing
  ; printed. A `*name` declares the intent, and the call emits BLR.
  *fill = PeekI(services + #SVC_SEAM_HDR_BYTES + #SVC_SEAM_SLOT_FILL * 8)
  If *fill = 0
    ProcedureReturn -2
  EndIf
  rc = fill(#SVCCAP_THERMAL, #SEAM_THERMAL_READ, @ThermalAvsRead)
  If rc <> 0
    ProcedureReturn rc
  EndIf
  gAvsBase = device
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ModuleQuiesce(services, device) - put the hardware down.
;
;  THIS DRIVER HAS NOTHING TO PUT DOWN AND SAYS SO BY SUCCEEDING. It
;  never wrote a register, never enabled a clock and never started a
;  transfer; the AVS monitor runs whether anything reads it or not.
;  Forgetting the base is the whole of its teardown, and it matters: the
;  seam function is about to be withdrawn, and a copy of the pointer
;  held somewhere the monitor cannot see would otherwise find a driver
;  still willing to read a device it no longer owns.
;
;  A DRIVER WITH REAL STATE ANSWERS NON-ZERO WHEN IT CANNOT. The loader
;  refuses the unload and leaves the record ACTIVE, because a driver torn
;  out over its own objection leaves silicon mid-transaction.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ModuleQuiesce(services.i, device.i)
  gAvsBase = 0
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
